// DoView Engine V1.0.14.3 2026-04-29
// DoView® Planning — Dr Paul Duignan — doviewplanning.org
// Engine file: place in head tag, then call DoView.init({...}) from body
// Usage: load via script tag in head, then call DoView.init({...}) in body script
// V1.0.12.12: very small corrective build on top of V1.0.12.11. (Fix 1) Widens the main popup/modal further and reduces the popup top heading size. The main popup/modal width base is increased from 820px to 1200px (still divided by --popup-scale) so the full popup is visible without sideways truncation and right-side controls such as Add are reachable without horizontal scrolling. The popup top heading size (.modal-box h3) is reduced from 18px to 11px so it reads as only modestly larger than the popup body text rather than as a visually dominant banner. Board scale is unchanged. --popup-scale itself is unchanged. Tooltip, other chrome, and all non-popup behaviour are unchanged. No schema, state, link, read-only, or interaction changes. Existing saved boards continue to load correctly.
// V1.0.12.13: very small corrective build on top of V1.0.12.12. Implements four targeted fixes and bumps the version everywhere relevant. (Fix 1) Detail popup inner width — the inner edit/detail popup content now uses the available modal width, with explicit width:100% / max-width:none on .ep, width:100% on .ep-ta (already), and flex-wrap:wrap + max-width:100% on .ep-add and .ep-struct so right-side controls such as Add are reachable without horizontal scrolling and rows wrap rather than overflow. The .meq-add row inside detail modals also wraps. No outer .modal-box width change; no popup-scale change; no board-scale change. (Fix 2) Optional board-level Top right text — a short plain-text annotation/disclaimer (e.g. Draft, Illustrative only, Confidential) shown right-justified in the page-info bar across all pages, click-to-edit in editable boards, view-only in read-only copies, persisted with the board. New topRightText field on saved state, defaults to '' for backward compatibility. (Fix 3) Text-only Official DoView® Standards-Compliant Board Structure Badge — a non-editable text-only badge in the top-right of the orange header, on the second and third lines below the existing SEE. PLAN. DO.™ + version line. White text, white rounded border, orange background, no logo, no icon. Click opens a non-editable info popup containing the trademark-use explanation and two clickable links (doviewplanning.org/trademarkuse and doviewplanning.org/trademark). Travels with saved/exported boards and read-only copies. (Fix 4) Bottom attribution link target — the visible doviewplanning.org link in the very bottom attribution bar now targets https://doviewplanning.org/doviewboards (visible text unchanged). No schema-shape changes that break older boards: existing saved boards continue to load correctly (topRightText defaults to ''). No other behaviour changes.
// V1.0.13.0: small corrective build on top of V1.0.12.13. Implements four targeted UI cleanup fixes and bumps the version everywhere relevant. (Fix A) This–Then / How link popup overflow — the previous V1.0.12.13 popup-width fix targeted the .ep / detail-panel classes, but the remaining overflow problem was specifically the This–Then / How link popup, where the inner row containing the dropdown/select and the Add button could still push the Add button off to the right and require horizontal scrolling. The fix targets the actual link-popup rows that hold the picker + Add button: .tt-link-add and .how-link-add now have flex-wrap:wrap + max-width:100% (parallel to the .ep-add / .ep-struct / .meq-add fix in V1.0.12.13) so they wrap rather than overflow at narrow widths; .tt-link-section and .how-link-section are constrained to max-width:100%; and .tt-link-sel, .how-link-sel, and .meq-sel get min-width:0 + max-width:100% so the select can shrink below its widest option text rather than forcing the row wider than its container. The inner Link detail popup (linkDetailModal) inherits these so its picker rows also wrap and its Add button stays visible. No outer modal width change here; no popup-scale change; no board-scale change beyond Fix D below. No schema, state, link, read-only, or interaction changes. (Fix B) Training? link in the top utility/header — adds a modest "Training?" link after the existing "🔍 Search" link in the orange header. Clicking it opens https://doviewplanning.org/training in a new tab/window. Plain link only — no popup, no modal, no sales text inside the board. Tooltip reads "Want training? Get help using DoView Boards". Visible in normal editable boards and in read-only copies. Non-editable. Kept in line with the other top utility links and does not add any other promotional UI. (Fix C) Official DoView® badge popup trademark URL cleanup — the popup opened by clicking the Official DoView® Standards-Compliant Board Structure Badge previously ended with two clickable links: doviewplanning.org/trademarkuse and doviewplanning.org/trademark. The second URL (doviewplanning.org/trademark) is removed; only doviewplanning.org/trademarkuse remains as the final clickable link, still pointing to https://doviewplanning.org/trademarkuse. Badge wording, styling, placement, click behaviour, popup heading, and broader certification/badge behaviour are unchanged. (Fix D) Laptop-friendly default board scale — the default --board-scale is reduced from 2 to 1.35 so a newly opened board fits comfortably on typical laptop viewports (around 1366×768 / 1440×900) without the user having to immediately zoom out. Existing zoom/scale mechanism is preserved; the only change is the default value. --popup-scale is unchanged at 1.75; popups remain readable and not oversized. No layout redesign. No schema or state changes. Backward compatibility is preserved: existing saved boards continue to load correctly, read-only copies still work correctly, and unrelated behaviour is not changed by this build. The deferred 1.0.13.1 items (border colour in the details pane, expanded traffic-light states, Links vs Associations terminology sweep, clickable URLs in popup display text) are NOT implemented in this build.
// V1.0.13.1: small corrective build on top of V1.0.13.0. Implements four targeted UI/terminology fixes and bumps the version everywhere relevant. (Fix 1) Border colour control in the box details pane — adds a compact Border color control row in the entry panel for This–Then, How, and Final Outcomes boxes, sitting near the existing Status (traffic light) and Priority controls. Presets only (no free-form colour picker): Default, Black, Grey, Orange, Blue, Green, Red, Purple. Default clears the custom border colour and returns the box to its normal style. The custom border colour applies only in the normal/resting state — selected, highlighted, reveal, inspect, search, and ghost/context states still override it where needed. The borderColor field is already plumbed through buildInitialState, saveState, boxHTML, howBoxHTML, rebuildBoxKeys, rebuildFinalKeys, and the AI chat state, so this is purely a UI addition; no schema change is needed. Custom border colour persists after save/reload and travels with read-only copies, where it remains visible but is not editable (the picker is hidden in read-only mode by the existing .ep-controls / read-only gating). (Fix 2) Two intermediate traffic-light states — adds Green/yellow and Yellow/red as additive states, displayed as vertically split circles (left half green, right half yellow for greenYellow; left half yellow, right half red for yellowRed). The full set is now Green, Green/yellow, Yellow, Yellow/red, Red, Grey/unset. Internal values are 'greenYellow' and 'yellowRed'; existing 'green', 'yellow', 'red', 'grey' continue to load and save correctly; existing boards saved before this build still load correctly. The two new states are rendered via a small CSS background-image gradient and applied consistently in the on-box traffic-light dot, the details-pane traffic-light picker, the on-box left-click chooser, and the AI chat state-text border/light readout. Read-only copies show the new states but the chooser/picker is gated as before. The cascade/auto-light logic is unchanged for the existing four states; the two new states are user-set only and are not produced by automatic cascade. (Fix 3) Use links for structural box-to-box relationships and associations for Measures / Evaluation Questions — a targeted user-facing wording sweep. Structural box-to-box relationships keep links / linked terminology (This–Then links, How / up-and-down links, non-up-and-down links). Measures and Evaluation Questions connected to boxes or to structural links now use associated / association terminology in user-facing wording: Link Measure → Associate Measure, Link Evaluation Question → Associate Evaluation Question, Linked Measures → Associated Measures, Linked Evaluation Questions → Associated Evaluation Questions, Unlink → Remove association (where it refers to a Measure/EQ), Link to box / Link to this box (where the subject is a Measure/EQ) → Associate with box, Link to link (where the subject is a Measure/EQ) → Associate with structural link. This is applied across the details pane, Measure/EQ popups, registries, link-detail Measures/EQ sections, headings, helper text, tooltips, and AI chat help wording. Internal code symbols (function names like linkMeasureToBox, action commands like linkMeasure / unlinkMeasure, internal CSS classes) are intentionally left unchanged to avoid unnecessary churn — the wording change is user-facing only. The top Links menu now lists structural links only and adds a Non-up/down badge for non-up-and-down structural links (existing How upward/downward keep the How badge). The Other links wording is not used. (Fix 4) Clickable URLs in popup display text — when user-entered text is shown in display/view mode in popups (the This–Then / How link detail popup, the Measure detail popup, the Evaluation Question detail popup, and the Board info / Page info popups), URLs are rendered as clickable links via the existing linkify helper. Implemented as a small read-only display block placed above the corresponding textarea, shown only when the saved field has content. Editable textareas are not turned into clickable link displays — the textarea remains the edit surface. Stored text is not altered; only its rendered display picks up linkify. Links open in a new tab/window using the existing target=_blank convention. The broader security-hardening safeUrl/sanitiser work and any other deferred items are NOT implemented in this build.
// V1.0.13.2: very small corrective build on top of V1.0.13.1. Implements two targeted fixes and bumps the version everywhere relevant. (Fix 1) Board-chat populated-page reliability — when the user asks the board chat to add and populate a new This–Then page (e.g. "add a page about X", "add a new This–Then page about X", "populate it", "add DoView This–Then boxes", "create a This–Then logic for X"), the board chat must now create a meaningful populated page rather than a one-box placeholder. The fix has three small, targeted parts and is NOT a full board-chat redesign. (a) The board-chat system prompt (buildBoardStatePrompt) now distinguishes blank/starter page requests from populated This–Then page requests, and explicitly requires that a populated This–Then page be emitted in ONE response with at least 3 columns, at least 6 boxes, compact DoView outcome phrasing, column headings that name causal stages (not generic "Stage 1, Stage 2"), and a plausible left-to-right This→Then causal flow with later/higher-level outcomes on the right. The prompt also gives a worked example so the AI predicts the new page id (p<SP.length+1>) and emits the full set of addSubpage + addColumn + addBox actions together rather than emitting addSubpage alone. (b) processAIResponse now detects populated-page intent in the user message via a small regex helper (isPopulatedThisThenPageRequest) and, if the AI's structural actions for a new this_then page contain fewer than 3 columns or fewer than 6 boxes, rejects the change rather than applying a one-box placeholder; a system message in the chat asks the AI/user to retry with full population. Blank/starter requests (explicitly using "blank", "starter", "empty", "page shell", or similar) bypass this validation and create a blank/starter page as before. (c) showConfirmation now renders a clean structured summary that groups addSubpage + addColumn + addBox actions for each new page and shows the page title, the column headings, and the actual box count, so the user sees what is being proposed before clicking OK. After OK, the engine applies the FULL set of pendingStructural actions (addSubpage + addColumns + addBoxes) as before — the proposed columns and boxes are carried through to the apply step. Existing pendingStructural / confirmStructural / execAction code paths are otherwise unchanged. No schema change. Existing saved boards continue to load correctly. Other board-chat actions (state changes, removals, renames, How-page actions, link/measure/EQ actions, etc.) behave as before. The later full board-chat structured-action redesign and the later security-hardening build remain deferred. (Fix 2) Popup scale set to 1.25 — after V1.0.13.0 (Fix D) reduced the default --board-scale to 1.35 to fit a normal laptop screen, popup windows scaled by --popup-scale=1.75 became a bit too large for that smaller board context. The default --popup-scale is now 1.25 so popups read at a more proportionate size relative to the board. --board-scale is unchanged at 1.35; popup layout, modal width rules, popup-content widths, popup-content typography, and the overall scaling system are unchanged. Popup text remains readable. No schema, state, link, read-only, or interaction changes.
// V1.0.14.0: main board-chat structured-action reliability build on top of V1.0.13.2. Implements seven targeted fixes to make the internal board chat more reliable when deciding whether to answer a question, ask a clarification question, or propose board edits, especially when creating new pages. NOT a security-hardening build, NOT the review-only fragility audit, NOT a broad UI redesign. (Fix 1) Board-chat intent classification — buildBoardStatePrompt now instructs the AI to classify the user request before generating any actions, distinguishing at least: (1) question / explanation only, (2) state-only edit, (3) structural edit, (4) blank This–Then page creation, (5) populated This–Then page creation, (6) How page creation, (7) Documentation page creation, (8) ambiguous page creation that requires clarification, (9) invalid or incomplete action proposal. Pure questions/explanations must be answered without proposing structural edits. The classification is invisible to the UI; it just drives behaviour reliably. (Fix 2) Ambiguous page creation asks for page type — when the user asks to add/create/make a page but does not clearly specify the page type and does not clearly imply a populated This–Then page, the board chat asks: "What type of page do you want? - This–Then page — shows what you want to happen in the world, and what leads to what. - How page — shows what is going to be done. - Documentation page — holds supporting documentation, notes, or rich-text material about the DoView Board." It does NOT create the page yet. Trigger phrasings include "add a page called X", "create a new page about X", "make a page for X", "add X as a page". Phrasings that bypass the question include explicit type words, "blank/starter/empty/page shell" with a type, and the populated triggers covered by Fix 3. New helper functions isPageCreationRequest, isExplicitPageType, and isAmbiguousPageCreationRequest classify the request at the engine side. processAIResponse now rejects any addSubpage emitted for an ambiguous request before queuing pendingStructural so no page is silently created. (Fix 3) "Full DoView" and related phrases trigger populated This–Then page behaviour — isPopulatedThisThenPageRequest now also triggers on "full DoView", "complete DoView", "full logic", and "full populated page", so phrasings such as "add a new page called leadership development which includes a full DoView on it", "add a complete DoView page about leadership development", "create a full logic for leadership development", and "add a full populated page about leadership development" reliably propose a populated This–Then page with columns and boxes rather than a one-box placeholder. The previous broad "add a page about X" matcher (without an explicit this-then keyword or populated trigger) is removed, so a bare "add a page about X" is now classified as ambiguous and asks for the page type. Explicit type wins: "make a Documentation page with a full DoView" still respects the explicit Documentation page type. (Fix 4) Domain-shaped page structure reminder for board chat — buildBoardStatePrompt now explicitly tells the AI that when creating a populated This–Then page, the number of columns and the number of boxes in each column must reflect the actual causal structure of the domain. Use as many causal stages as are genuinely needed; vary the number of boxes per column according to how many distinct outcomes/conditions/enabling steps exist at that stage; column headings must name real causal stages (not "Stage 1 / Stage 2 / Inputs / Activities / Outputs" unless those are genuinely the best domain-specific headings). The 3 columns / 6 boxes minimum is a floor, not a target — do NOT default to a mechanical 3x2 or 3x3 template. (Fix 5) Clearer OK / Cancel preview for structural actions — _buildStructuralChangeSummaryHTML now also shows a Populated / Blank/starter status pill for This–Then page proposals, so the OK / Cancel preview makes obvious whether a full page is being proposed or only a shell. Page title, page type, populated/blank status, column count, column headings, and box count are all visible before clicking OK. (Fix 6) Validation before applying structural changes — _findInsufficientThisThenPageProposals now also flags populated This–Then page proposals whose column headings are mechanical numbered placeholders (Stage 1 / Stage 2, Column 1 / Column 2, Step 1 / Step 2, Phase 1 / Phase 2) and rejects them with a clear message. Existing populated-page validation (≥ 3 columns, ≥ 6 boxes) remains. processAIResponse runs validation before snapshot, before any state-action execution, and before showing OK / Cancel — no page is partially applied. For How and Documentation pages, validation does not force any populated structure; existing safe behaviour is preserved. (Fix 7) Existing board-chat behaviour preserved — state-only edits, status changes, priority changes, border colour behaviour, box text edits, page renaming, column renaming, single-box adds, structural confirmation, read-only gating, saved/exported board behaviour, Create Read-Only Copy behaviour, board search, Measures and Evaluation Questions, association wording from V1.0.13.1 (Fix 3), This–Then and How links, and the popup-scale value from V1.0.13.2 (Fix 2) are unchanged. No schema change. Backward compatibility preserved: existing saved boards continue to load correctly. The deferred items (security/privacy hardening, safeUrl helper, documentation HTML sanitising, API key persistence changes, startup active-content warning, broad board-chat architecture changes beyond this targeted reliability work, read-only annotation mode, broader permissions/access-control systems, further stable page-type indicators in page tabs, This–Then links between This–Then boxes including across This–Then pages with full UI for cross-page edges, How links upward to higher-level How boxes beyond what the current adjacent-level system supports, board-level Measures and Evaluation Questions UI beyond what already exists, Documentation Page clones of selected board elements beyond what already exists, page-title clones, stable numbering for How boxes / Measures / Evaluation Questions beyond the current schemes, clone references stored lightly rather than duplicating source text, richer distributed/share modes beyond simple read-only copies, annotation/comment modes on top of read-only copies, clearer public standards/specification wording for DoView Board structures) are NOT implemented in this build.
// V1.0.14.1a: small corrective UI / read-only clarity build on top of V1.0.14.0. Implements five small, self-contained user-visible clarity fixes and bumps the version everywhere relevant. NOT a security-hardening build, NOT the larger board-chat validation/transaction build, NOT a unique-IDs build. (Fix 1) Read-Only Copy label and tooltip — when a board is opened as a read-only copy, a modest "Read-Only Copy" label appears left-justified under the board title (moved from the right-hand side of the header). The label uses an honest tooltip "Editing is disabled through the board interface. This is not security protection; someone with technical skills could still edit the HTML." so users are not misled into thinking read-only copies are tamper-proof. The label is visually modest (small, neutral, single line) and does not look like a warning banner or security guarantee. The label only appears in read-only copies (gated by body.is-readonly) — normal editable boards do not show it. The CSS rule for .ro-label is updated to render under the title in .hdr-left rather than as a pill in .hdr-right. Existing read-only gating behaviour is unchanged; saved/exported behaviour is unchanged; isReadonly:true continues to drive read-only mode. (Fix 2) Documentation Page wording updated — wherever the user sees a Documentation Page description as part of choosing a page type, the wording now reads exactly "Documentation page — holds supporting notes, explanation and basic rich-text documentation about the DoView Board.", replacing the older "Documentation page — holds supporting documentation, notes, or rich-text material about the DoView Board." This is a wording correction only; no advanced clone features are promised here, no Documentation Page features are broadened, and the engine continues to use the existing safe behaviour for Documentation pages. The wording change appears in the board-chat clarification question (buildBoardStatePrompt) and any other place in the engine where the user sees a list of page-type descriptions for choosing. (Fix 3) Sources URL clickability — the Sources popup gains an "Open" link beside each non-empty URL in editable boards so the saved URL can be opened in a new tab without entering edit mode, while the title and URL inputs remain editable. In read-only copies, source rows render as view-only — the source title is shown as plain text, the URL is shown as a clickable "Open" link (or, if no title is set, the URL itself is shown as the link), and editable inputs, Add source, Save, and Remove are hidden; the Close button remains so the popup can be dismissed. The sources data shape is unchanged; existing saved boards continue to load correctly; broader URL/safeUrl hardening remains deferred to the later security build. (Fix 4) Top right text affordance — when topRightText is empty, the engine no longer shows the modest "+ Add top right text" affordance in the right-hand page-info bar; this affordance is hidden in both editable boards and read-only copies. When topRightText is non-empty, the existing right-justified display behaviour is preserved (clickable in editable boards to open the existing Top right text editor, plain non-clickable in read-only copies). To preserve a low-key way to add or edit Top right text from a sensible existing place, the Board info edit mode now exposes a compact single-line "Top right text (optional)" field that edits the existing topRightText saved-state value. The topRightText saved-state field is unchanged; existing saved boards continue to load correctly; the data shape is unchanged. (Fix 5) Board info / Page info view-edit pattern — Board info and Page info popups now open in rendered view mode by default in editable boards. The saved text is rendered once with clickable URLs (via the existing linkify display block), an Edit button is shown, and the editable textarea is hidden until the user clicks Edit. When Edit is clicked, the textarea appears and Save / Cancel controls are shown; Save commits and returns to view mode, Cancel discards unsaved changes and returns to view mode (the previous saved text is preserved). Empty fields show a modest empty-state line with an Edit button rather than a duplicate textarea-plus-rendered-block. In read-only copies, only the rendered view is shown — Edit, Save, and the textarea are hidden, and URLs in the rendered view remain clickable. Board info edit mode also includes the compact "Top right text (optional)" field from Fix 4 (Board info only — Page info does not show this field). The saved data shape is unchanged; existing saved boards continue to load correctly; this is not a Documentation Page-style rich-text editor; broader sanitiser/security hardening remains deferred. (Version) The version is now V1.0.14.1a everywhere relevant: the engine first-line comment, the visible board version brand line in the orange header ("See. Plan. Do.™ V1.0.14.1a"), and the print-view brand line. Backward compatibility preserved: existing saved boards continue to load correctly, read-only copies still work correctly, normal editable boards remain editable where they should, and unrelated behaviour is not changed by this build.
// V1.0.14.1b: corrective fragility build on top of V1.0.14.1a. Focuses on board-chat validation, transaction handling, and safer action processing, plus three small UI tweaks. NOT a unique-IDs build, NOT a security-hardening build, NOT a broad architecture change. The next planned build (likely V1.0.14.2) is the stable unique-ID build; security hardening comes after that. (Fix 1) Default Sources entry — when a board has no explicit sources state (fresh board, or a board that pre-dates this build with no saved sources), the engine seeds a single default source { title: "Information on DoView Boards", url: "https://doviewplanning.org/doviewboards" } so the Sources popup is never empty/unfinished-looking. The seed runs once on init when sources is empty AND the new sourcesInitialized flag is not set in the loaded saved state. After seeding, sourcesInitialized is set true and persisted, so the user's later edits/removals of the default source are respected — the default is NOT re-added on reload. The sources data shape (array of {title,url}) is unchanged; sourcesInitialized is a new additive optional saved-state flag that defaults to false for older boards (backward compatible). The seed never duplicates an existing default URL. (Fix 2) Sources URL input no longer misleads with grey "https://" — the URL input previously used placeholder="https://..." which looked like an automatic prefix that would be applied to whatever the user typed (it was not). The placeholder is now "Enter full URL, e.g. https://example.org" and a small helper line ("Enter the full URL, including https://") sits inside the Sources popup so the user understands they should type the complete URL. URL editability and the "Open" affordance from V1.0.14.1a (Fix 3) are unchanged in editable boards, and read-only Sources rows remain view-only. The engine does NOT silently prepend https:// or rewrite user-entered URLs; broader safeUrl hardening remains deferred to the later security build. (Fix 3) Read-Only label is now a modest pill inline with the top utility links — replaces the V1.0.14.1a quiet text label under the title. The label text is "Read-Only Board" (replacing "Read-Only Copy" in the on-screen label); it sits inline with the top utility links such as "Training?" in the orange header; it is styled as a modest pill (subtle outline, small horizontal padding, slightly larger font than the previous tiny text but still fitting the header), not a warning banner; it does not look like a clickable utility link; it carries the exact tooltip "Editing is disabled through the board interface. This is not security protection; someone with technical skills could still edit the HTML."; and it appears only in read-only copies (gated by body.is-readonly). The label markup moves out of .hdr-title-wrap and back into the row of top utility links inside .hdr-left, and the now-unused .hdr-title-wrap reverts to a simple title row. There is exactly one read-only label in the UI (no duplicate "Read-Only Copy" elsewhere). Existing read-only gating behaviour is unchanged; this is a UI-only mode indicator and not a security guarantee. (Fix 4) Plain-language OK/Cancel previews for destructive actions — _buildStructuralChangeSummaryHTML now renders friendly, descriptive lines for individual structural actions such as removeBox, removeColumn, removeSubpage, removeFinalOutcome, deleteMeasure, deleteEQ, removeTTLink, removeHowLink, unlinkMeasure, unlinkEQ. Examples: "Remove box \"Funding secured\" from page \"Leadership Development\"."; "Remove column \"Early Conditions\" from page \"Leadership Development\"."; "Remove page \"Leadership Development\"."; "Remove final outcome \"Community wellbeing improved\"."; "Remove association between Measure \"Attendance rate\" and box \"Participation increased\"."; "Remove structural link from \"Funding secured\" to \"Programme delivered\"." If the engine cannot resolve the target object (for example a missing box id), the action is rejected by Fix 5 (preflight validation) before OK / Cancel is shown. The OK / Cancel pattern itself is unchanged. (Fix 5) Preflight validation for structural actions and for all action targets — a new helper _validateActionSet runs in processAIResponse BEFORE snapshot, BEFORE any state-action execution, and BEFORE showing OK / Cancel. It checks that every proposed action references a real or earlier-in-set page, column, box, Measure, Evaluation Question, final outcome, structural link, or association; that page types in addSubpage are supported (this_then, how, documentation); that column indices are valid for the resolved page; that new labels/headings are non-empty; that addBox actions have valid page and column targets; and that the action type itself is supported by the engine (matches the dispatch in execAction). If any action fails validation, the WHOLE action set is rejected: nothing is applied, no snapshot is taken, no OK / Cancel is shown, and the user receives a concise plain-language rejection message. Examples of rejection messages: "I could not apply that change because the target box could not be found. No changes were made."; "I could not apply that change because the action format was incomplete or malformed. No changes were made."; "Unsupported action: \"moveMeasure\". I could not apply that change. No changes were made."; "I could not add that box because the target column could not be found. No changes were made." This validation is conservative — it does NOT replace the existing populated-page validation (_findInsufficientThisThenPageProposals), which still runs alongside it. (Fix 6) Mixed state + structural changes apply as one transaction — when a chat response contains BOTH state changes (setLight, addNote, etc.) and structural changes (addBox, removeColumn, etc.), the engine no longer applies state changes immediately and structural changes after OK. Instead, both kinds are queued and the OK / Cancel preview shows them together; on OK, all changes are applied as one transaction; on Cancel, none are applied. State-only responses still apply directly as before (preserving the existing safe direct-apply behaviour); structural-only responses still go through OK / Cancel as before. The implementation uses a small new pendingStateActions queue alongside the existing pendingStructural queue and a snapshot taken before any apply, so Cancel rolls back to a clean state without partial application. (Fix 7) Extended phrase coverage for board chat without over-broadening — isPageCreationRequest now also detects "make/add another tab", "set up a section", "build out a page", and "turn this into a full page" as page-creation requests; isPopulatedThisThenPageRequest now also detects "do/make the full map", "map this out", "build the full logic", "build out this page", "fill out this page", and "turn this into a full page" as populated triggers. Ambiguous page creation (no explicit type, no populated trigger) continues to ask the page-type clarification question. Question-only requests still answer in plain language with no edits. Broad words like "map" and "section" only trigger when used in tight phrasings (e.g. "set up a section for X", "do the full map for X"); they do NOT trigger automatic edits in question-only contexts. The existing V1.0.14.0 triggers ("full DoView", "complete DoView", "full logic", "full populated page", and the legacy populated triggers) all continue to work unchanged. (Fix 8) Stronger populated This–Then page validation — _findInsufficientThisThenPageProposals continues to enforce the ≥3 columns / ≥6 boxes floor and continues to reject mechanical numbered headings (Stage 1 / Stage 2, Column 1 / Column 2, Step 1 / Step 2, Phase 1 / Phase 2). It now also flags a populated This–Then page proposal whose column headings are dominated by the generic logic-model template Inputs / Activities / Outputs (specifically: 2 or more of those generic words used as headings together), so a mechanical "Inputs / Activities / Outputs" page is rejected unless the AI uses a domain-specific shape. Single occurrences of those words are allowed (a domain may genuinely have one stage called "Outputs"). The minimum is still treated as a floor, not a target. (Fix 9 / Fix 10) Validation requires valid page and column targets for new boxes, and valid targets for ALL action shapes — addBox actions whose target page does not exist (current SP or an earlier addSubpage in the same set) are rejected; addBox actions whose target column does not exist (current sp.cols OR an earlier addColumn in the same set targeting that page, OR for a new this_then page the implicit "First Stage" default first column at index 0) are rejected; removeBox / renameBox / removeColumn / renameColumn / moveColumn / removeSubpage / removeFinalOutcome / deleteMeasure / deleteEQ all require their target to resolve in current state or earlier in the same set. Positional references (pX, pX-cY, pX-cY-bZ, final-bI, MNNN, EQNNN) continue to be the references used; this build does NOT introduce stable unique IDs. (Fix 11) Malformed action tags handled safely — if any [ACTION:...] tag is parsed but its argument count is below the minimum for that command, or it has an unknown/garbled command name, the whole action set is rejected with a plain-language message rather than silently applying the rest. Internal parsing errors are not exposed; raw stack traces never reach the chat. (Fix 12) Unsupported action types rejected clearly — if an [ACTION:...] tag uses a command name the engine does not implement (for example "moveMeasure", "addHowBox" — which is described in some legacy prompt wording but is not currently dispatched in execAction), the whole action set is rejected with a clear "Unsupported action: ..." message. The engine does NOT add support for new action types in this build. (Fix 13) Improved rejection messages — every rejection path uses short, plain-language wording, always states explicitly that no changes were made, and never blames the user. The wording is consistent with the per-fix examples above. (Version) The version is now V1.0.14.1b everywhere relevant: the engine first-line comment, the visible board version brand line in the orange header ("See. Plan. Do.™ V1.0.14.1b"), and the print-view brand line. Backward compatibility preserved: existing saved boards continue to load correctly, the saved-state schema is unchanged apart from the additive optional sourcesInitialized flag (which defaults to false for older boards), read-only copies still work correctly, normal editable boards remain editable where they should, and unrelated behaviour is not changed by this build. The deferred items (stable unique IDs for board objects, broad URL/safeUrl hardening, sanitiser replacement, active-content startup warnings, Documentation Page rich-text sanitisation, API key storage redesign, public share security redesign, Documentation Page clones beyond what already exists, page-title clones, stable numbering for How boxes / Measures / Evaluation Questions, lightweight clone references, richer distributed/share modes, annotation/comment modes, broader public standards/specification wording, further stable page-type indicators in page tabs, cross-page This–Then links UI, broader How upward link UI) are NOT implemented in this build.
// V1.0.14.2: stable unique IDs build plus two small link/detail-popup fixes on top of V1.0.14.1b. Conservative additive build. NOT a security-hardening build, NOT a broad board-chat hardening build, NOT a multi-file architecture refactor. (Fix 1) Stable unique IDs for all significant board objects — the board, pages/subpages, columns, boxes (This–Then, How, Final Outcomes), structural links (This–Then and How), Measures, and Evaluation Questions all now carry an additive `uid` field that is generated once, persists through save / load / Save HTML Board / Copy HTML Board / Create Read-Only Copy / localStorage / DOVIEW-STATE snapshots, and does not change when the object is renamed, moved, reordered, or edited. The uid is independent of array position or the existing positional keys / ids (pX, pX-cY-bZ, final-bI, H001, M001, EQ001, ttl_*, hwl_*) and does NOT replace them. (Fix 2) ID format — _genUid produces a globally unique stable ID with a short type prefix (board_, page_, col_, box_, final_, link_, measure_, eq_) using crypto.randomUUID() where available and a Date.now()+Math.random() fallback otherwise. The exact format is intentionally simple; what matters is stability and uniqueness. (Fix 3) Loading old boards without uids — _backfillUids runs once on init() after savedState restoration AND at the top of saveState() so any object that lacks a uid is assigned one before being persisted. Visible content, page order, column order, box order, final outcomes, Measures, EQs, and links are all unchanged during backfill. Existing positional keys, ttLink/howLink ids, and M/EQ ids continue to work. After backfill, saveState persists the new uids so they are not regenerated on reload. (Fix 4) Save / load / export / copy preserve uids — the cleanB serializers in saveState, copyBoardHTML, downloadBoard (Save HTML Board), createReadOnlyCopy, and doCreateAndSaveNewBoard all now include the per-box uid (and optional sourceUid / sourceBoardUid provenance fields if present). The savedState wrapper additionally includes boardUid. JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(SP)) etc. naturally preserve sp.uid, col.uid, sp.howBoxes[].uid, ttLinks[].uid, howLinks[].uid, measures[].uid, and evalQuestions[].uid because they live on the same in-memory objects. (Fix 5) New objects get uids at creation — buildInitialState seeds uids on every box / column / How box / page / final outcome it constructs from initial config. Beyond that, _backfillUids is also invoked at the start of saveState() so any object created by any UI / board-chat / execAction path that did not assign a uid inline will receive one before the next persistence write — a uid is never absent from saved/exported/copied state. Board-chat actions continue to use the existing positional / id action syntax; the AI is not required to supply or reference uids. (Fix 6) Board uid and copy / read-only copy semantics — the board itself now carries a stable boardUid (additive, separate from the existing boardInstanceId positional/identity field). Save HTML Board and Copy HTML Board preserve the same boardUid because they are saved versions of the same board. Create Read-Only Copy preserves the same boardUid AND the same per-object uids (the least disruptive choice for this build), so a read-only copy and its source share the same uids. The existing boardInstanceId continues to be mutated with the `_ro_<ts>` suffix on read-only copies as before. (Fix 7) Optional provenance fields — sourceUid and sourceBoardUid are accepted on objects that already carry them in loaded state and are preserved through serialization. They are NOT populated for ordinary existing objects in this build, NOT shown in the UI, and NOT required. Cross-board import, clone references, and a provenance UI remain deferred. (Fix 8) Internal logic remains backward-compatible — current positional references, page/column/box keys, ttLink/howLink ids, and M/EQ ids are all still in use throughout the engine, board chat, and saved state. The board-chat action syntax is unchanged; existing saved boards still load; visual ordering and labels are unchanged. (Fix 9) Detail popup duplication removed in editable mode — openLinkDetail, openMeasureDetail, and openEQDetail no longer render a separate clickable display block above the editable textarea/input in editable boards. Editable detail popups now show the editable field only — single edit surface, no duplicate rendered preview, no Edit button. In read-only copies, the same popups now render the saved text as a read-only display block (with clickable URLs) and hide the editable textarea/input. Board info and Page info popups continue to use their existing view/edit pattern from V1.0.14.1a (Fix 5) and are NOT changed by this build. (Fix 10) Consistent Delete link option for all structural link types — the link detail popup now has a clear "Delete link" button at the bottom in editable boards only, for both This–Then links and How links (including up-and-down and non-up-and-down classifications). Clicking it asks "Delete this link from \"<source>\" to \"<target>\"?" and on OK removes only the structural link (and any link-level Measure/EQ associations attached to that link), leaves the source box, target box, and board-level Measures/EQs untouched, saves state, closes the popup, and re-renders. On Cancel, nothing happens. The Delete link button is hidden in read-only copies. The Links registry continues to render rows that open the link detail popup, where Delete link is now consistently available — this satisfies the "registry opens detail popup where Delete link is available" path described in the build. Existing per-row × delete affordances on box-detail link rows (removeTTLink / removeHowLink) remain unchanged. (Version) The version is now V1.0.14.2 everywhere relevant: the engine first-line comment, the visible board version brand line in the orange header ("See. Plan. Do.™ V1.0.14.2"), and the print-view brand line. Backward compatibility preserved: existing saved boards continue to load correctly (uids are backfilled additively on load), the saved-state schema is unchanged apart from the additive optional uid / boardUid / sourceUid / sourceBoardUid fields, read-only copies still work correctly, normal editable boards remain editable where they should, and unrelated behaviour is not changed by this build. The deferred items (broad URL/safeUrl hardening, sanitiser replacement, active-content startup warnings, Documentation Page rich-text sanitisation, API key storage redesign, public share security redesign, Documentation Page clones beyond what already exists, page-title clones, stable numbering for How boxes / Measures / Evaluation Questions, cross-board import, cross-board link UI, board-chat shift to uid-based references, broader provenance UI, richer distributed/share modes, annotation/comment modes, broader public standards/specification wording, further stable page-type indicators in page tabs, cross-page This–Then links UI, broader How upward link UI, and the wider security build) are NOT implemented in this build.
// V1.0.14.3: small additive build on top of V1.0.14.2 introducing multi-profile AI Connection Settings with an optional CORS proxy per profile. NOT a security-hardening build, NOT a board-chat behaviour change, NOT a unique-IDs change. (Fix 1) Multi-profile AI Connection Settings — replaces the single ⚙️ AI Connection Settings form in the chat panel with a list of named connection profiles, each holding its own endpoint, API key, model, and (optional) CORS proxy URL. Exactly one profile is marked active at a time via a radio toggle on its card; aiSettings always mirrors the active profile so the existing sendChat() / chat-status code keeps working unchanged. Adds new globals aiProfiles (array of {id,name,endpoint,key,model,proxyUrl}), activeProfileId (string|null), and editingProfileId (string|null), plus profile lifecycle functions addNewProfile, editProfile, deleteProfile, setActiveProfile, cancelProfileEdit, and onRememberToggle, all exposed on window for inline onclick use. (Fix 2) Profile persistence to localStorage — profiles persist as a single JSON record under doview_ai_profiles ({profiles:[...], activeId}) when the existing "Remember profiles in this browser" checkbox is ticked; unticking removes both the new doview_ai_profiles key and the legacy doview_ai_settings key. (Fix 3) Backward-compatible migration on load — init reads doview_ai_profiles first; if absent and a legacy doview_ai_settings record exists, migrates it forward into a single "Saved profile" and writes it back as doview_ai_profiles; if neither exists and the board's embedded config supplies aiEndpoint/aiModel, seeds a "Board default" profile (no key). aiSettings is then populated from the active profile via _applyActiveAIProfile. Existing saved boards continue to load correctly. (Fix 4) Optional CORS proxy URL per profile — a new optional proxyUrl string on each profile lets browser-based DoView clients reach providers that lock CORS to same-origin (e.g. Sovereign Engine, some self-hosted gateways) and would otherwise fail with a "Failed to fetch" network error. The new helper _proxiedUrl(endpoint, proxy) routes the fetch URL through the proxy when set: if proxy contains the literal {url}, it is replaced with the URL-encoded target endpoint; otherwise the proxy prefix is concatenated to the endpoint. testChatConn and sendChat both use _proxiedUrl. Behaviour for OpenAI / OpenRouter / Groq / Together / Ollama is unchanged when proxy is left blank. The "Failed to fetch" error in testChatConn now appends a hint pointing the user at the proxy field when a proxy isn't already set. (Fix 5) Profile editor UX — the chat settings pane now shows a profile list (radio + name + endpoint + model + optional "via CORS proxy" tag, plus Edit and Delete buttons per row), a "+ New profile" button that opens an inline editor, and the editor with Save profile / Test Connection / Cancel. Test Connection works whether you are creating or editing and also routes through the proxy when set. Saving an edited or new profile sets that profile as active. Deleting the active profile picks the first remaining profile as active or clears aiSettings if none remain. (Fix 6) API keys remain browser-only — saved/exported HTML embeds only aiEndpoint and aiModel of the active profile via the existing copyBoardHTML / downloadBoard / read-only copy / new-empty-board paths. No API keys, no proxy URLs, and no other profiles travel with exported HTML. (Fix 7) Status header reflects the active profile — chat header now reads "✓ Connected: <profile name>" while the chat is using a profile, and falls back to "Click ⚙️ to connect an AI" when no profile is active and no platform-side claudeConnected probe has succeeded. (Version) The version is now V1.0.14.3 everywhere relevant: the engine first-line comment, the visible board version brand line in the orange header ("See. Plan. Do.™ V1.0.14.3"), and the print-view brand line. Backward compatibility preserved: existing saved boards continue to load correctly, the saved-state schema is unchanged (no new top-level fields embedded in exported HTML), read-only copies still work correctly, normal editable boards remain editable where they should, and unrelated behaviour (board chat, structural validation, unique IDs from V1.0.14.2, etc.) is not changed by this build. The deferred items previously listed under V1.0.14.2 — broad URL/safeUrl hardening, sanitiser replacement, active-content startup warnings, Documentation Page rich-text sanitisation, API key storage redesign (e.g. encryption at rest in the browser), public share security redesign, Documentation Page clones beyond what already exists, page-title clones, stable numbering for How boxes / Measures / Evaluation Questions, cross-board import, cross-board link UI, board-chat shift to uid-based references, broader provenance UI, richer distributed/share modes, annotation/comment modes, broader public standards/specification wording, further stable page-type indicators in page tabs, cross-page This–Then links UI, broader How upward link UI, and the wider security build — are NOT implemented in this build.

